# An allowlist, not a denylist. Everything is excluded, and the image gets back
# only what it needs. A denylist has to anticipate every private file, while an
# allowlist fails closed: a new file under local_content/ or a new
# config.<env>.yaml never reaches the build context, let alone the image.
#
# What must never be in an image, since an image is a thing people push:
# licensed catalogs (local_content/, BYOC HITRUST and GovRAMP), secrets (.env),
# the organization's own config and topic registry, and generated drafts
# (output/). tests/test_container.py fails if one of those becomes reachable.
*

!pyproject.toml
!README.md
!LICENSE
!requirements/runtime.txt
!src/
# The four public catalogs by name. HITRUST and GovRAMP are bring-your-own
# and licensed: only their READMEs come in, so a controls.json someone copied
# into data/frameworks/hitrust-csf/ by hand still stays out.
!data/frameworks/nist-800-53-r5/
!data/frameworks/fedramp/
!data/frameworks/arc-ampe/
!data/frameworks/hipaa-security-rule/
!data/frameworks/*/README.md
!config/config.example.yaml
!config/topics.example.yaml

# Re-excluded inside what was let back in.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/*.egg-info
data/frameworks/crosswalk.json
